fixes=[
{'id':'A','kind':'missing linearity / type ambiguity','locators':['thm:estimated_packet_reconstruction','thm:estimated_packet_gibbs','cor:reconstruction_after_detection'],'source_context':'Notation says bounded approximation operator; paragraph before retained identity says Q:X->X bounded. Linearity is not explicit. The retained identity itself does not need linearity, but the estimated-packet identity does.','counterexample':'X=R, Q(x)=|x|, s=1, j=-1, jhat=0. Q is norm bounded by 1. f-fhat=0 while (I-Q)s+(I-Q)(j-jhat)=-2.','repair':'State that every Q_m used in estimated-packet reconstruction is a bounded linear operator. Add linear to notation and relevant theorem hypotheses.','repaired_argument':'Linearity gives Q_m(s+j-jhat)=Q_ms+Q_m(j-jhat). Triangle inequality and the linear operator norm give the advertised bound.','impact':['estimated packet identity','estimated packet norm bound','post-detection reconstruction','estimated Gibbs bound']},
{'id':'B','kind':'actual false claim if constants unconstrained','locators':['prop:nonunitary_tree_bounds'],'source_context':'Local displayed inequalities impose no nonnegativity on A_v, B_v. The induction multiplies by child lower constants.','counterexample':'Two successive zero maps on R along a root to child to terminal unary tree, both with A_v=-1 and B_v=0. Each local inequality -|x|^2<=0<=0 holds. Recursive lower constant at root is (-1)(-1)=1, but leaf output is zero for x=1.','repair':'Require 0<=A_v<=B_v<infinity. Strict A_v>0 is needed only when asserting a positive frame lower bound.','repaired_argument':'All child a(w) are nonnegative by induction, so multiplying local lower bounds preserves inequalities. Upper constants are nonnegative as well.','impact':['nonunitary recursive bounds only; unitary pruning and additive optimization unaffected']},
{'id':'C','kind':'step-domain convention conflict','locators':['thm:haar_step_coefficient','cor:haar_scale_separation','cor:two_grid_haar_detection'],'source_context':'Earlier H_tau=1_[tau,1]. Haar subsection moves to R or a chosen extension but does not prescribe the endpoint continuation.','counterexample':'Use earlier bounded-interval step H_{1/4}=1_[1/4,1], amplitude 1, shifted Haar support [1/2,3/2] of length 1, positive on [1/2,1], negative on [1,3/2]. The coefficient is 1/2 despite the support not crossing tau. The extra discontinuity at 1 is responsible.','repair':'In the Haar subsections explicitly use H_tau(t)=1_[tau,infinity)(t) on R. Its restriction to (0,1) agrees with the earlier step. Any endpoint extension must use the declared half-line model on the analyzed supports.','repaired_argument':'Each half-line step is constant on a support excluding its sole jump; direct integration produces the triangular coefficient. The half-cell shift chooses a support at distance at least L/4 from its endpoints. L below minimum separation excludes other jumps.','impact':['Haar step coefficients','scale threshold gap','two-grid detection; no claim of deduplicated recovery or synthesis for the redundant system']},
{'id':'D','kind':'omitted radius range','locators':['thm:geometric_packet_bound'],'source_context':'Definition of linear tube bound supplies the estimate only for 0 < delta < delta0; theorem does not repeat that range.','counterexample':'For the unit circle in R^2, mu(N_delta)=4 pi delta for 0 < delta < 1. Thus C=4 pi,delta0 = 1 qualifies. With E=unit disk, F=disk of radius 3.9, delta=3, E triangle F is inside N_3 but its area 14.21 pi exceeds C delta = 12 pi.','repair':'Require 0 < delta < delta0, where delta0 is the radius in the linear tube bound.','repaired_argument':'Symmetric-difference inclusion then invokes the available tube estimate in its stated range, followed by exact scalar region formula.','impact':['geometric mask bound; boundary-band corollary already says small delta']},
{'id':'E','kind':'convergence-mode clarification','locators':['cor:probability_consistency'],'source_context':'The section supplies independent centered sub-Gaussian noises and the earlier fixed jump model. Final sentence says packet error converges to zero without a mode.','counterexample':None,'repair':'Say convergence in probability in every finite L^p norm, for the fixed finite model and admissible sampling grids above. Do not claim almost sure convergence.','repaired_argument':'For each tolerance e > 0 and confidence delta > 0 the deterministic bound on the good event is eventually below e. Hence limsup P(error>e)<=delta. Delta is arbitrary.','impact':['probability consistency wording']},
{'id':'F','kind':'make inherited sampling scope explicit','locators':['thm:deterministic_jump_recovery','cor:deterministic_consistency','cor:probability_consistency'],'source_context':'Recovery section already assumes a Lipschitz background, no jump on a sample, at most one per cell. The minimum amplitude is defined only for a nonempty jump list.','counterexample':'Tau=1/2 lies on samples for every evenn. Thus an asymptotic assertion over all integern cannot silently use the no-jump-on-grid assumption.','repair':'State Q>=1 when defining min amplitude;Q=0 is trivial if handled separately. In asymptotic statements explicitly retain fixed nonzero amplitudes, finite Q, positive separation and admissible grids.','repaired_argument':'On those grids, separation eventually gives at most one jump per cell and the original threshold proof applies.','impact':['clarifies inherited asymptotic domain; no threshold constant changed']},
{'id':'G','kind':'tie and singleton boundary clarification','locators':['thm:best_basis','cor:best_basis_selection_stability'],'source_context':'Finite packet-tree context is explicit. Original recursion only specifies strict comparison branches. g>0 tacitly presupposes a competitor.','counterexample':'A one-node tree has no second smallest pruning cost. Equal stop and split costs leave both strict reconstruction branches unspecified.','repair':'Retain on ties using c(v)<=sum child F; split otherwise. Require a competing pruning for gap definition, or state singleton trivially stable. Define g = min_{L!=L*}(C(L)-C(L*)).','repaired_argument':'Either tie choice attains the displayed minimum; stop-on-ties makes the algorithm total. For competitors, each gap decreases by at most 2epsilon_star.','impact':['selection rule completeness, no alteration of optimal cost formula']},
{'id':'H','kind':'inherited independence clarification','locators':['cor:repeated_measurements'],'source_context':'Sub-Gaussian subsection already assumes independent noises across sample sites. Repeated-measurements corollary adds independent repetitions.','counterexample':None,'repair':'Optionally make all site and repetition noises independent explicit. This is a clarification of inherited context, not an identified contradiction.','repaired_argument':'Averages at separate sites remain independent with variance proxy sigma^2/R; adjacent differences have proxy 2 sigma^2/R.','impact':['probability confidence convention only']},
{'id':'I','kind':'delay-unit convention clarification','locators':['thm:perfect_reconstruction','thm:imperfect_reconstruction'],'source_context':'Section explicitly operates in relevant polyphase spaces, so z^{-d} is a channel delay. Text calls it input delay without units.','counterexample':'N=2 and scalar multiplierz^{-1} on both channels produces a two-fine-sample shift, not a one-fine-sample shift.','repair':'Specify d coarse-channel samples; this corresponds to N d fine samples in the N-phase one-dimensional setting.','repaired_argument':'Inverse channel transform shifts each channel by d, and interleaving multiplies the fine-address offset by N.','impact':['interpretation of reconstruction delay, no matrix identity changed']},
{'id':'J','kind':'external standard-fact scope','locators':['sf:gibbs_behavior'],'source_context':'Statement says isolated jump without a local regularity class; proof is citation only.','counterexample':None,'repair':'Restrict contextual Gibbs statement to a periodic piecewise C1 function with an isolated nonzero jump. Keep explicitly outside proved-result/Lean denominator. Authenticate an exact source separately if claiming fresh literature verification.','repaired_argument':'Standard classical Gibbs asymptotics apply under this regularity; retained-packet theorems themselves only use the exact algebraic error identity and do not depend on this fact.','impact':['contextual imported fact only; no downstream proof dependency']}
]
fixes.extend([
{'id':'K','kind':'zero noise boundary case','locators':['thm:probability_recovery'],'source_context':'The stated moment-generating-function proxy permits sigma=0, but the displayed tail proof divides by sigma^2.','counterexample':'The stated proof expression is undefined at sigma=0. The theorem itself remains valid.','repair':'Handle sigma=0 separately: a 0-sub-Gaussian centered variable is zero almost surely, so the deterministic contrast bound applies with zero noise; thereafter assume sigma>0 for the Chernoff division.','repaired_argument':'For sigma=0, Markov gives P(eta>=t)<=exp(-lambda t) for every lambda > 0 and t > 0; let lambda tend to infinity and repeat for -eta.','impact':['probability proof boundary case; recovery constant unchanged']},
{'id':'L','kind':'false prose assertion about actual noise maxima','locators':['rem:noise_scaling'],'source_context':'Remark says the maximum noise contrast grows slowly with n at fixed scale. Sub-Gaussianity only supplies an upper-tail certificate, not actual growth.','counterexample':'Independent Rademacher noises taking values plus/minus sigma are sigma-sub-Gaussian, while every adjacent noise contrast is bounded by 2 sigma for all n.','repair':'Say the displayed uniform high-probability certificate scales like sigma*sqrt(log n), so this certificate does not yield consistency at fixed sigma. Decreasing effective noise or repeated averaging supplies sufficient regimes.','repaired_argument':'This describes the bound actually proved and makes no unsupported lower-growth claim for general sub-Gaussian distributions.','impact':['noise-scaling explanatory remark only; no recovery theorem invalidated']}
])
fixes.append({'id':'M','kind':'cross-part complex inner-product convention','locators':['thm:smooth_haar_bound'],'source_context':'The latest 20-result core declares conjugate-linear first slot. Annals standalone does not specify that convention but its smooth-Haar proof writes an un-conjugated s-integral. On integration into a common final paper, the displayed equality must follow the common convention.','counterexample':'With s(t)=i*t on [0,L] and real normalized Haar, the un-conjugated integral has the opposite sign to the inner product under a conjugate-linear first slot. Magnitudes are identical.','repair':'Put a complex conjugate over s(t)-s(t+L/2) in the pairing integral, or explicitly use an equivalent consistent inner-product ordering.','repaired_argument':'The modulus of the conjugated difference is unchanged, so ML^(3/2)/4 and every subsequent absolute coefficient estimate stay exactly the same.','impact':['one proof equality under combined-edition conventions; no numerical threshold or theorem conclusion changed']})
